      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says three things:\n1. If George likes music, he wants to compose.\n2. If George has access to a program, he can compose.\n3. If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose.\n\nThe question is: Based on this, is the statement \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose.\" True, false, or uncertain?\n\nHmm. Let me break this down. First, I need to understand the given premises and then see how they relate to the statement in question.\n\nPremise 1: If George likes music, he wants to compose. So, liking music leads to wanting to compose. But it doesn't say anything about whether he can compose or not. It just sets a condition for wanting to compose.\n\nPremise 2: If George has access to a program, he can compose. So, having a program allows him to compose. Again, it's a condition for being able to compose.\n\nPremise 3: If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose. This seems like a logical statement: (Can compose \u2227 Wants compose) \u2192 Will compose.\n\nNow, the statement in question is: If George will not compose, George cannot compose. So, in logical terms, that's \u00acWill compose \u2192 \u00acCan compose.\n\nWait, but in logic, the contrapositive of \"If P, then Q\" is \"If \u00acQ, then \u00acP,\" which is logically equivalent. So, if we have \"If (Can compose \u2227 Wants compose), then Will compose,\" the contrapositive would be \"If \u00acWill compose, then \u00ac(Can compose \u2227 Wants compose).\" Which means, if he doesn't want to compose, then he can't compose or doesn't want to compose. But the statement in the question is simpler: If he won't compose, then he can't compose. That's a bit different.\n\nWait, let me think again. The statement is \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose.\" So, \u00acWill compose \u2192 \u00acCan compose. But from premise 3, we have (Can compose \u2227 Wants compose) \u2192 Will compose. The contrapositive is \u00acWill compose \u2192 \u00ac(Can compose \u2227 Wants compose). Which means, if he doesn't compose, then either he can't compose or doesn't want to compose. But the statement in the question is only about not being able to compose, not about not wanting to compose.\n\nSo, the statement in the question is a stronger claim. It says that not composing implies not being able to compose, but from the premises, we only know that not composing implies not both being able and wanting to compose. So, it's possible that George doesn't want to compose, but he can still not compose for other reasons. But the statement in the question is specifically about not being able to compose, not not wanting to compose.\n\nWait, but the statement is \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose.\" So, it's saying that not composing implies not being able to compose. But from premise 3, we have that if he can compose and wants to compose, he will. So, if he doesn't compose, it could be because he can't compose, or because he doesn't want to. But the statement in the question is only about the case where he doesn't compose, and concludes he can't compose. But that's not necessarily true because he might not want to compose even if he can. So, the statement is making a claim that's not necessarily supported by the premises.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm overcomplicating it. Let me try to model it with logic symbols.\n\nLet me define:\n- L: George likes music\n- W: George wants to compose\n- P: George has access to a program\n- C: George can compose\n- K: George will compose\n\nPremises:\n1. L \u2192 W\n2. P \u2192 C\n3. (C \u2227 W) \u2192 K\n\nQuestion: Is \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC true?\n\nWait, the statement is \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose.\" So, \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC.\n\nBut from premise 3, we have (C \u2227 W) \u2192 K. The contrapositive is \u00acK \u2192 \u00ac(C \u2227 W), which is \u00acK \u2192 (\u00acC \u2228 \u00acW). So, if he doesn't compose, then either he can't compose or doesn't want to compose.\n\nBut the statement in the question is \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC, which is a stronger statement than what we have. Because \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC is equivalent to C \u2192 K, which is the converse of premise 3. Wait, no, premise 3 is (C \u2227 W) \u2192 K, which is different from C \u2192 K.\n\nWait, no, the contrapositive of (C \u2227 W) \u2192 K is \u00acK \u2192 \u00ac(C \u2227 W), which is \u00acK \u2192 (\u00acC \u2228 \u00acW). So, if he doesn't compose, then either he can't compose or doesn't want to compose.\n\nBut the statement in the question is \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC, which would mean that if he doesn't compose, then he can't compose. But from the premises, we only know that if he doesn't compose, then either he can't compose or doesn't want to compose. So, it's possible that he doesn't want to compose but can still compose. Wait, no, because if he can compose, he would compose only if he wants to. Wait, no, the premises don't say that. Let me think again.\n\nPremise 3 says that if he can compose and wants to compose, he will. But it doesn't say anything about if he can compose but doesn't want to compose. So, he might be able to compose but not want to, in which case, even if he can compose, he won't compose because he doesn't want to. So, in that case, \u00acK would be true because he doesn't compose, but C would still be true because he can compose. Therefore, \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC would be false because \u00acK is true, but \u00acC is false.\n\nSo, in that scenario, the statement in the question would be false. Therefore, the statement is not necessarily true based on the premises. So, the answer would be uncertain, but wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, the statement is \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose.\" So, \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC. But from the premises, we have \u00acK \u2192 (\u00acC \u2228 \u00acW). So, if \u00acK is true, then either \u00acC or \u00acW is true. But the statement in the question is that \u00acK implies \u00acC, which would require that \u00acK leads to \u00acC, but it's possible that \u00acK leads to \u00acW instead. Therefore, the statement in the question is not necessarily true because it's possible that George doesn't compose because he doesn't want to, even if he can compose. Therefore, the statement is false because it's possible for George to not compose without being unable to compose.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused. Let me try to model it with an example.\n\nSuppose George can compose (C is true) but doesn't want to compose (W is false). Then, according to premise 3, since he can compose but doesn't want to, he won't compose (K is false). So, in this case, \u00acK is true, and \u00acC is false. Therefore, the statement \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC would be false because the antecedent is true, and the consequent is false.\n\nTherefore, the statement is false because there's a scenario where George doesn't compose (\u00acK) but can still compose (C is true), making \u00acK \u2192 \u00acC false.\n\nSo, the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose\" is false because it's possible for George to not compose without being unable to compose. He might not want to compose even if he can, which would make the statement false.\n\nAnswer: B) False",
