nFrom premise 5, if someone has good parent relationships, they do not eat salads regularly. But wait, in this case, Taylor does eat salads regularly because of premise 2. So there's a contradiction here. If Taylor has good parent relationships, she shouldn't eat salads, but premise 2 says she does. That's a problem. So Case 1 leads to a contradiction. Therefore, Case 1 cannot be true. So Taylor cannot be in Case 1.\n\nThat leaves us with Case 2: Taylor neither grew up in a health-conscious home nor has good parent relationships.\n\nSo Taylor did not grow up health-conscious, and she does not have good parent relationships.\n\nFrom premise 4, if someone has good parent relationships, they fulfill their nutritional daily intakes. But in this case, Taylor does not have good parent relationships, so premise 4 doesn't apply. However, premise 3 says that if someone fulfills their nutritional daily intakes, they grew up health-conscious. But Taylor did not grow up health-conscious, so she must not have fulfilled her nutritional daily intakes. Because if she had, she would have to have grown up health-conscious, which she didn't.\n\nSo Taylor does not fulfill her nutritional daily intakes.\n\nNow, looking at premise 6: If someone visits the gym at least once a day, they always fulfill their daily nutritional intakes. The contrapositive of this is that if someone does not fulfill their daily nutritional intakes, they do not visit the gym at least once a day.\n\nSince we established that Taylor does not fulfill her daily nutritional intakes (from premise 3 and the fact that she didn't grow up health-conscious), then according to premise 6's contrapositive, Taylor does not visit the gym at least once a day.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Premise 6 says gym visit leads to fulfilling nutritional intakes. So if Taylor doesn't fulfill them, she didn't go to the gym. So Taylor does not visit the gym at least once a day.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Taylor visits the gym at least once a day\" is false.\n\nI think that's the conclusion. Let me just go through again to make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\n- Taylor is in Case 2: no health-conscious home, no good parent relationships.\n- Therefore, she doesn't fulfill her nutritional intakes (from premise 3).\n- From premise 6, not fulfilling intakes means she didn't go to the gym (contrapositive).\n- So the statement is false.\n\nYes, that seems correct.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is false because Taylor does not fulfill her nutritional intakes, which means she does not visit the gym.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
