jects of worship, but it's also possible that only some are. The given information doesn't specify.\n\nWait, no. Let me clarify. If some objects of worship are fir trees, and all fir trees are evergreens, then those objects of worship are also evergreens. So, at least some evergreens are objects of worship. But the statement is about whether some evergreens are not objects of worship. That's the opposite.\n\nSo, the statement is saying that there exists at least one evergreen that is not an object of worship. But from the given information, we can't conclude that. It's possible that all evergreens are objects of worship, or only some are. We don't have enough information to say for sure.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The second statement says some objects of worship are fir trees. So, the objects of worship include at least some fir trees, which are evergreens. But does that mean that all objects of worship are evergreens? No, because the first statement only says all fir trees are evergreens, not that all evergreens are fir trees.\n\nWait, no, the first statement is about fir trees being a subset of evergreens. The second is about some objects of worship being fir trees, which are evergreens. So, the objects of worship include some evergreens (the fir trees), but we don't know if they include all evergreens or just some.\n\nSo, the statement \"Some evergreens are not objects of worship\" would be true if there are evergreens outside the set of objects of worship. But since we don't know that, it's possible that all evergreens are objects of worship, or only some are. Therefore, the statement is uncertain because we can't conclude it's true or false based on the given information.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused. Let me try to visualize it with a Venn diagram.\n\nImagine a big circle labeled Evergreens. Inside that, a smaller circle labeled Fir Trees. Now, another circle labeled Objects of Worship overlaps with the Fir Trees circle. So, the overlap between Obje