      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand everything.\n\nThe context says:\n1. Some basketball players are not American.\n2. All basketball players are tall.\n3. If someone is tall, they wear large shoes.\n4. If someone wears large shoes, they wear large-sized clothes.\n5. Yuri does not wear large-size clothes.\n\nThe question is: Based on this information, is the statement \"If Yuri does not wear large shoes, then Yuri is an American basketball player.\" True, false, or uncertain?\n\nAlright, let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's outline the given information:\n\n- Some basketball players are not American. So, there's a subset of basketball players who are American, and another subset who are not. But we don't know how big these subsets are.\n\n- All basketball players are tall. So, every basketball player, regardless of being American or not, is tall.\n\n- If someone is tall, they wear large shoes. So, being tall is a necessary condition for wearing large shoes. In logical terms, tall \u2192 large shoes.\n\n- If someone wears large shoes, they wear large-sized clothes. So, large shoes \u2192 large clothes.\n\n- Yuri does not wear large-size clothes. So, \u00aclarge clothes.\n\nNow, the statement we need to evaluate is: If Yuri does not wear large shoes (\u00aclarge shoes), then Yuri is an American basketball player (Yuri is American \u2227 Yuri is a basketball player).\n\nLet me try to represent this in logical terms.\n\nLet me denote:\n- B = being a basketball player\n- A = being American\n- T = being tall\n- S = wearing large shoes\n- C = wearing large-sized clothes\n\nFrom the context:\n1. Some B are not A. So, \u2203x (Bx \u2227 \u00acAx). But this doesn't tell us much about all B, except that not all are A.\n2. All B are T. So, B \u2192 T.\n3. T \u2192 S. Because if someone is tall, they wear large shoes.\n4. S \u2192 C. If someon