      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement \"Black Mirror\" is popular based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that \"Stranger Things\" is a popular Netflix show. That's given as a fact. Now, the next part says that if a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. So, since \"Stranger Things\" is popular, Karen must be binge-watching it. \n\nThen, it says that Karen will download a show only if and only if she binge-watches it. The phrase \"if and only if\" is important here because it means it's a biconditional statement. So, Karen downloads a show exactly when she's binge-watching it. She doesn't download shows she doesn't binge-watch, and she doesn't binge-watch shows she doesn't download.\n\nNext, it mentions that Karen does not download \"Black Mirror.\" So, since she doesn't download it, according to the previous statement, she also doesn't binge-watch it. \n\nNow, it's given that \"Black Mirror\" is a Netflix show. So, if Karen doesn't binge-watch it, then it's not a popular show because the first statement says that only popular shows get binged by Karen. \n\nWait, let me make sure I'm not mixing anything up. The first statement is \"If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.\" That doesn't necessarily mean that all shows Karen binges are popular, but it's a one-directional implication. However, the second part is \"If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it.\" So, that's a biconditional, meaning that downloading and binge-watching are equivalent for her. \n\nSince Karen doesn't download \"Black Mirror,\" she doesn't binge-watch it. Therefore, \"Black Mirror\" isn't a show she's binged on. But the first statement says that if a show is popular, she will binge it. But it doesn't say that if she binges it, it's popular. Wait, no, actually, the first statement is \"If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.\"