y, but it doesn't say that all ugly people are evil. So, being ugly doesn't necessarily mean you're evil. So, Harry could be evil, but he could also be kind. But since he's a gentleman, and all gentlemen are handsome, and the context says a man is either kind or evil, but being a gentleman doesn't necessarily mean he's kind. So, Harry could be kind, but he could also be evil.\n\nWait, but if Harry is a gentleman, and all gentlemen are handsome, and the context says a man is either kind or evil, then being a gentleman doesn't necessarily mean he's kind. So, Harry could be kind, but he could also be evil. So, he could be either.\n\nNow, the statement is \"Harry is kind or a CEO.\" Let's see if this is necessarily true, false, or uncertain.\n\nWe know Harry is a gentleman, so he's not a CEO unless he's both a gentleman and a CEO. But the context says all CEOs are gentlemen, so being a CEO makes you a gentleman. But being a gentleman doesn't necessarily make you a CEO. So, Harry could be a CEO, but it's not required. So, he could be a CEO or not.\n\nBut the statement is \"Harry is kind or a CEO.\" So, if Harry is a CEO, then the statement is true. If he's not a CEO, but he's kind, then the statement is also true. However, if he's neither kind nor a CEO, then the statement is false.\n\nBut wait, we know Harry is a gentleman, so he's not evil. Because if he were evil, he would be ugly, but being a gentleman means he's handsome, so he can't be evil. Wait, no, that's not necessarily the case. Wait, the context says all evil people are ugly, but it doesn't say that all ugly people are evil. So, Harry could be evil, but that would make him ugly, but being a gentleman means he's handsome, so he can't be evil. Wait, that's a contradiction. Because if Harry is a gentleman, he's handsome, so he can't be evil because evil people are ugly, and being a gentleman means he's not ugly. So, actually, Harry cannot be evil because being a gentleman means he's not ugly, and evil people are