But the question is about wings. The only thing we know is that if something can fly, it has wings. So, Rock can't fly, but that doesn't tell us anything about whether it has wings or not. It could have wings or not; the information doesn't specify.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Rock does not have wings\" is uncertain because the given information doesn't provide enough evidence to conclude that Rock doesn't have wings. It only tells us that Rock doesn't fly, but having wings is independent of flying, except that flying requires wings. So, Rock could have wings or not, but we don't know for sure.\n\nWait, but the statement in the context says that if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it neither flies nor breathes. So, Rock doesn't fly, and it doesn't breathe. But the question is about wings. The only thing we know is that if something can fly, it has wings. So, Rock can't fly, but it might have wings or not. The context doesn't say anything about non-animals having wings or not. So, we can't conclude anything about wings based on the given information.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Rock does not have wings\" is uncertain because the information doesn't provide enough to confirm or deny that Rock has wings.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Rock does not have wings\" is uncertain because the given information doesn't provide enough evidence to confirm or deny that Rock has wings. \n\nAnswer: C) Uncertain",
