\n- From premise 3: F.\n- From premise 5: Y could be true or false, but from premise 2: U is false.\n\nSo, in this case, Ben is TS, F, not U, and Y could be true or false.\n\nIn Case 2: Ben is F.\n\n- From premise 1: P.\n- From premise 2: U is false.\n- From premise 3: If L, then F. But since he's F, L could be true or false.\n- From premise 4: If TS, then L. But in this case, we don't know if he's TS or not because he's F, but premise 6 says he's either TS or F. So, he could be TS or not TS.\n\nWait, but if he's F, he could be TS or not TS. If he's TS, then from premise 4, he's L, which would make him F. If he's not TS, then he's not L, but still F.\n\nBut from premise 5: Y \u2192 TS. So, if he's Y, he must be TS. But in this case, he's F, so if he's Y, he's TS, but we don't know if he's Y or not.\n\nSo, in this case, Ben is F, not U, and Y could be true or false, but if Y is true, he must be TS, which is possible because he's F.\n\nWait, but in Case 2, he's F, so he could be Y or not Y.\n\nIf he's Y, then he's TS (from premise 5), but he's also F. So, he's F and TS, which is possible because premise 6 allows him to be either TS or F.\n\nBut from premise 2: If he's U, then he's not P. But he's F, so he's P, so he can't be U.\n\nSo, in both cases, U is false.\n\nSo, regardless of whether Ben is TS or F, U is false.\n\nNow, let's look at Y.\n\nIn Case 1: Ben is TS, so from premise 5, Y could be true or false.\n\nIn Case 2: Ben is F, so Y could be true or false, but if Y is true, he must be TS (from premise 5). But in this case, he's F, so if he's Y, he's TS, but he's also F, which is allowed.\n\nWait, but in Case 2, if he's Y, he's TS, but he's F, so he's both TS and F. But from premise 6, he's either TS or F, so that's fine.\n\nSo, in both cases, Y could be true or false.\n\nBut from premise 2, U is false in both cases.\n\nSo, Ben is not U.\n\nNow, the statement is: (Y \u2227 U) \u2228 (\u00acY \u2227 \u00acU).\n\nSince U is false, let's substitute U with F.\n\nSo, 