ir parents are busy. So, if Peter's household is high net income, parents are busy, which would make Peter an only child, which contradicts Scenario 2. Therefore, Peter's household cannot be high net income. So, Peter's household is not high net income.\n\nBut does that affect whether he tidies and enjoys? Not directly, because statement 3 only applies to only children, and Peter isn't one. So, in Scenario 2, Peter could be a non-only child and either tidy and enjoy or not. Therefore, in this scenario, we can't conclude for sure whether he tidies and enjoys.\n\nBut wait, the question is whether the statement \"Peter tidies his space consistently and enjoys the process of cleaning\" is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nIn Scenario 1, Peter does not tidy and enjoy.\n\nIn Scenario 2, we can't conclude for sure whether he does or not.\n\nSo, overall, we have two possibilities: either he doesn't tidy and enjoy (Scenario 1), or he might or might not (Scenario 2). Therefore, the statement could be true or false depending on the scenario. So, it's uncertain.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. In Scenario 2, Peter doesn't spill and isn't an only child. From statement 3, only children tidy and enjoy, but Peter isn't an only child, so he might not. But could he still choose to tidy and enjoy? The statements don't provide any information about non-only children's habits regarding tidying. So, it's possible, but not certain.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Peter tidies his space consistently and enjoys the process of cleaning\" could be true or false based on the scenario. So, the answer is uncertain.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is uncertain because Peter could be in either scenario where he either doesn't tidy and enjoy (Scenario 1) or might or might not (Scenario 2). \n\nAnswer: C) Uncertain",
