rk remotely. So, in this case, he doesn't have lunch at home and doesn't work remotely. So, the first part of the conclusion in the statement is false, but the second part is true. Wait, the statement says he either has lunch at home and works remotely, or neither has lunch at home nor works remotely. In this case, he doesn't have lunch at home and doesn't work remotely, so the second option is true. So, the conclusion holds.\n\nCase 2: James is in other countries.\n\nFrom point 5, if he's in other countries, he works remotely. From point 6, no managers work remotely, so he can't be a manager. Therefore, James is in other countries, works remotely, but is not a manager. Now, from point 3, he either has lunch in the company or at home. If he's in other countries, does that affect where he has lunch? Wait, point 3 says employees either have lunch in the company or at home. So, being in other countries doesn't directly say where he has lunch. But from point 2, if he has lunch in the company, he schedules meetings and appears. But if he's in other countries, does he have lunch at home?\n\nWait, let's think. If he's in other countries, he works remotely. From point 4, if he has lunch at home, he works remotely. But he's already working remotely because he's in other countries. So, does that mean he could have lunch at home or in the company? Wait, but if he's in other countries, he's not in the company, so he can't have lunch in the company. Therefore, he must have lunch at home. Because from point 3, he must have lunch either in the company or at home. Since he's in other countries, he can't be in the company, so he must have lunch at home.\n\nTherefore, if he's in other countries, he has lunch at home and works remotely. So, the first part of the conclusion in the statement is true: he has lunch at home and works remotely.\n\nSo, in both cases, whether James is a manager or in other countries, the conclusion holds. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. If James is a manager, he appears in the company, has lunch there, so he doesn't have lunch at home and doesn't work remotely. So, he falls into the second part of the conclusion: neither has lunch at home nor works remotely.\n\nIf James is in other countries, he works remotely and has lunch at home, so he falls into the first part of the conclusion: has lunch at home and works remotely.\n\nTherefore, in both scenarios, the conclusion is true. So, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is true because in both cases\u2014whether James is a manager or in another country\u2014the conclusion holds based on the given conditions.\n\nA) True\n\nAnswer: A",
