      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nFirst, the context has several statements. Let me break them down one by one.\n\n1. If people spill a lot of food on their clothing, then they do not tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy the process of cleaning. So, Spilling food \u2192 Not tidying consistently or enjoying cleaning.\n\n2. All foodies who go out frequently to find new food restaurants to try are people who spill a lot of food on their clothing. So, Foodie and frequent eaters \u2192 Spilling a lot.\n\n3. If people grew up as only children with no siblings, then they tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy the process of cleaning. So, Being an only child \u2192 Tidying consistently and enjoying cleaning.\n\n4. If people have parents who are very busy with their regular jobs, then they grew up as only children with no siblings. So, Busy parents \u2192 Only child.\n\n5. All households with a high net income have people who have parents who are very busy with their regular jobs. So, High net income households \u2192 Busy parents.\n\nNow, the question is about Peter. The statement says Peter is either both a person who spills a lot of food and is an only child, or he is neither. So, Peter is in one of two categories: (a) Spills food and is an only child, or (b) Doesn't spill food and isn't an only child.\n\nThe question asks whether Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently and belongs to a high net income household, or he is neither a foodie nor a high net income household. So, we need to determine if Peter must be in one of these two scenarios.\n\nLet me try to map this out.\n\nFrom statement 2, foodies who go out frequently are those who spill a lot of food. So, if Peter is a foodie who goes out frequently, he must spill a lot of food. From statement 1, if he spills a lot, then he doesn't tidy consistently and doesn't enjoy cleaning. But from statement 3, if someone is an only child, they do tidy consistently and enjoy cleaning. So, if Peter is an only child, he must be tidying and enjoying, which contradicts the fact that he's a foodie who goes out frequently (since that would mean he doesn't tidy). Therefore, Peter cannot be both a foodie who goes out frequently and an only child because that would create a contradiction.\n\nWait, but the question says Peter is either both (spills and is an only child) or neither. So, if he is an only child, he must be a foodie who goes out frequently, but that leads to a contradiction because being an only child means he tidies, which contradicts the foodie's behavior. Therefore, Peter cannot be an only child. So, the only other option is that he is neither a foodie nor an only child.\n\nBut wait, let me think again. If Peter is an only child, he must be a foodie who goes out frequently because of statement 2. But that leads to a contradiction because being an only child means he tidies, which contradicts the foodie's behavior. Therefore, Peter cannot be an only child. So, the only possibility is that he is neither a foodie nor an only child.\n\nBut the question is asking whether Peter is either (a) a foodie and high net income or (b) neither foodie nor high net income. So, if Peter is neither a foodie nor an only child, does that mean he is neither a foodie nor a high net income household?\n\nWait, let's see. From statement 4 and 5, if a household has busy parents, the people in it are only children. And from statement 3, only children tidy and enjoy cleaning. But how does that relate to net income?\n\nStatement 5 says all high net income households have busy parents. So, if Peter's household is a high net income household, then the people in it have busy parents, so they are only children. But from statement 3, only children tidy and enjoy, which contradicts statement 1 because if they are only children, they should be tidying, but if they are foodies, they are not tidying. So, if Peter is in a high net income household, he would be an only child, which would mean he is a foodie, but that's a contradiction. Therefore, Peter cannot be in a high net income household either.\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me try to map it step by step.\n\nFirst, Peter is either:\n\nOption 1: Spills food and is an only child.\n\nOption 2: Doesn't spill food and isn't an only child.\n\nIf Option 1 is true, then from statement 2, he is a foodie who goes out frequently. But from statement 3, being an only child means he tidies and enjoys cleaning. However, statement 1 says that if he spills food, he doesn't tidy and enjoy cleaning. So, being an only child and a foodie leads to a contradiction because he can't both tidy and not tidy. Therefore, Option 1 is impossible. So, Peter must be in Option 2: Doesn't spill food and isn't an only child.\n\nNow, if Peter is not an only child, he could have siblings. From statement 4, if someone has busy parents, they are only children. So, if Peter is not an only child, his parents are not busy, or he has siblings. But statement 5 says that all high net income households have busy parents, meaning that if Peter's household is high net income, his parents are busy, making him an only child. But we've established that Peter is not an only child, so his household cannot be high net income. Therefore, Peter's household is not a high net income household.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Peter is neither a foodie nor in a high net income household. Therefore, the statement in the question is true because Peter is neither a foodie who goes out frequently nor in a high net income household.\n\nWait, but the question is asking if Peter is either (a) a foodie and high net income or (b) neither foodie nor high net income. From our reasoning, Peter is neither, so the statement is true because he is in category (b). Therefore, the answer should be A) True.\n\nBut wait, let me double-check. If Peter is not an only child, then his parents are not busy (since busy parents make them only children). Therefore, his household is not high net income because high net income implies busy parents. So, Peter is not in a high net income household. Also, since he is not an only child, he is not a foodie (from statement 2). Therefore, he is neither a foodie nor in a high net income household, making the statement true.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is true because Peter is neither a foodie who goes out frequently to find new restaurants nor part of a household with a high net income.\n\nA) True\n\nAnswer: A",
