lane. So, if Greyhound is an airline, it could have a Boeing 707 as one of its planes. But from premise 5, if Greyhound is not an airline, then there are no Greyhound planes. So, if Greyhound is an airline, it can have planes, including Boeing 707s.\n\nBut wait, the question is whether Greyhound is a Boeing 707. So, is it possible for Greyhound to be a Boeing 707? Well, a Boeing 707 is a plane, so if Greyhound is an airline, it could have a Boeing 707 as one of its planes. But the question is whether Greyhound itself is a Boeing 707. That would mean Greyhound is both an airline and a plane, which is possible, but let's see.\n\nWait, no. If Greyhound is an airline, it's an organization, not a physical entity. So, it can't be a plane. Planes are physical objects, while airlines are organizations. So, Greyhound, as an airline, can't be a plane. Therefore, Greyhound cannot be a Boeing 707 because Greyhound is an airline, and a Boeing 707 is a plane, and an airline is not a plane.\n\nWait, but that's assuming that Greyhound is an airline. But what if Greyhound is not an airline? Then, from premise 5, there are no Greyhound planes. So, Greyhound can't have any planes, including a Boeing 707.\n\nSo, regardless of whether Greyhound is an airline or not, Greyhound cannot be a Boeing 707 because:\n\n- If Greyhound is an airline, it's an organization, not a plane.\n- If Greyhound is not an airline, then premise 5 says there are no Greyhound planes, so no Boeing 707s.\n\nTherefore, in either case, Greyhound cannot be a Boeing 707. So, the statement \"A Greyhound is not a Boeing 707\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any scenario where Greyhound could be a Boeing 707? Well, a Boeing 707 is a plane, so it's a physical object. Greyhound, if it's an airline, is an organization, not a physical object. So, it can't be a plane. If Greyhound is not an airline, then it can't have any planes, so no Boeing 707s.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe stat