lse, making the implication true.\n\nWait, but the question is about the truth of the statement, not about whether R(Breaking Bad) is true or false. The statement is an implication, and in logic, an implication is only false when the antecedent is true and the consequent is false.\n\nIn this case, if R(Breaking Bad) is false, then the implication is true because it's an implication with a false premise. So, the statement is true.\n\nBut wait, I'm getting confused. Let me clarify.\n\nThe statement is: If R(Breaking Bad), then Monday at 8 pm.\n\nIn logic, this is R(x) \u2192 M(x), where x is Breaking Bad.\n\nIf R(x) is false, then the implication is true regardless of M(x). So, since R(Breaking Bad) is false, the implication is true.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but the options are A) True, B) False, C) Uncertain.\n\nSo, the answer would be A) True.\n\nBut wait, I'm not sure. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is about whether the statement is necessarily true based on the given context. Since R(Breaking Bad) leads to a contradiction, R(Breaking Bad) must be false. Therefore, the implication is vacuously true.\n\nSo, the statement is true.\n\nBut I'm a bit confused because sometimes in logic, if the antecedent is impossible, the implication is considered true. So, yes, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but let me check the structure again.\n\nGiven that R(Breaking Bad) leads to RT(Breaking Bad), which is false, R(Breaking Bad) must be false. Therefore, the implication \"If R(Breaking Bad), then Monday at 8 pm\" is true because the antecedent is false.\n\nSo, the answer is A) True.\n\nBut wait, the options are A, B, C. So, I think the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but I'm not 100% sure. Let me try to think differently.\n\nSuppose roses are given out during Breaking Bad. Then, according to the context, every rose given out is on the Bachelor, so Breaking Bad is on the Bachelor. Then, everything on the Bachelor portrays real people, so 