ething is a domesticated animal, it's not an aligator. But Ted is an aligator, so Ted cannot be a domesticated animal. Therefore, Ted is not domesticated.\n\nNow, from statement 3, all cows are domesticated. So, if Ted were a cow, he would have to be domesticated. But we just concluded that Ted is not domesticated because he's an aligator. Therefore, Ted cannot be a cow. So, the premise \"Ted is a cow\" is false. Therefore, the implication \"If Ted is a cow, then Ted is not a pet\" is vacuously true because the premise is false.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether the statement is true, false, or uncertain. So, if Ted is a cow, then Ted is not a pet. But since Ted cannot be a cow (because he's an aligator and cows are domesticated, which are not alligators), the statement is true because the implication is true when the premise is false.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm overcomplicating it. Let's see:\n\nIf Ted were a cow, then he would be a bovine (from statement 1), hence domesticated (from statement 3). But since all domesticated animals are not alligators (statement 4), Ted cannot be an aligator. But Ted is an aligator (statement 5), so he can't be a cow. Therefore, the statement \"If Ted is a cow, then Ted is not a pet\" is true because the antecedent is false, making the implication true regardless of the consequent.\n\nWait, but the consequent is \"Ted is not a pet.\" But from statement 2, some pets are cows. So, it's possible that Ted is a pet, but since he's an aligator, and from statement 5, he is an aligator, but statement 2 says some pets are cows, but it doesn't say that all pets are cows. So, Ted could be a pet, but he's an aligator, which is not a cow, so he's not in the overlap. But the statement is about if Ted were a cow, then he's not a pet. But since he can't be a cow, the implication is true.\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting tangled up. Let me try to map it out.\n\nGiven:\n- All cows are bovines.\n- Some pets are cows. So, there's at least one pet that's a cow, but not necessarily all.\n- All bovines are domesticated. So, cows are domesticated.\n- No domesticated animals are alligators. So, alligators are non-domesticated.\n- Ted is an aligator, so Ted is non-domesticated.\n\nNow, if Ted were a cow, he would be a bovine, hence domesticated. But since he's an aligator, he can't be domesticated. Therefore, Ted cannot be a cow. So, the statement \"If Ted is a cow, then Ted is not a pet\" is true because the antecedent is false. In logic, an implication is true if the antecedent is false, regardless of the consequent.\n\nAlternatively, if Ted were a cow, he would be a domesticated animal, hence not an aligator, which he is. So, the statement holds.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether Ted is a cow implying he's not a pet. But since Ted is an aligator, he can't be a cow, so the implication is true because the premise is false.\n\nSo, the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is true because if Ted were a cow, he would have to be domesticated, which contradicts him being an aligator. Since the premise is false, the implication is true.\n\nA) True\n\nAnswer: A",
